<section class="source-text source-copy project-section" style="--source-space:96px;--source-height:99px" data-source-section="1351:2186"><div data-node-id="1351:2186" data-name="Copy / AN IMAGE IN SPACE" class="content-stretch flex gap-[60px] items-start min-h-[99px] relative shrink-0 w-[1344px]"><div data-node-id="I1351:2186;1112:493" data-name="Label" class="content-stretch flex flex-col items-start relative shrink-0 w-[330px]"><p data-node-id="I1351:2186;1112:494" class="[word-break:break-word] font-['Hanken_Grotesk'] font-bold font-bold leading-[1.6] relative shrink-0 text-[color:#0a0a0a] text-[length:12px] tracking-[0.96px] uppercase w-[300px]">AN IMAGE IN SPACE</p></div><div data-node-id="I1351:2186;1112:495" data-name="Copy" class="content-stretch flex flex-col items-start relative shrink-0 w-[954px]"><p data-node-id="I1351:2186;1112:496" class="[word-break:break-word] font-['Hanken_Grotesk'] font-normal leading-[1.5] relative shrink-0 text-[color:#0a0a0a] text-[length:22px] w-[930px]">The lenticular proposal takes the portraits beyond the screen. Layers of glass separate the image into depth, allowing it to align from one viewpoint and fracture from another. Movement, reflection and daylight become part of the composition.</p></div></div></section>
<SourceComposition width={842} height={340} spaceBefore={96} sourceNode="1480:1026" wide={false} align="center"><p data-node-id="1480:1026" style="font-feature-settings:&quot;salt&quot; 1, &quot;lnum&quot; 1, &quot;tnum&quot; 1" class="[text-box-edge:cap_alphabetic] [text-box-trim:trim-both] [word-break:break-word] font-['Space_Grotesk'] font-normal leading-[0] relative shrink-0 text-[100.11px] text-black w-[842px] whitespace-pre-wrap"><span class="font-['Space_Grotesk'] font-bold font-bold leading-[0.9]">// Experiments<br aria-hidden> in optics, </span><span class="leading-[0.9]">identity<br aria-hidden> &amp; perception.</span></p></SourceComposition>
<SourceComposition width={1450} height={1213} spaceBefore={96} sourceNode="1423:1872" wide={true} align="center"><div data-node-id="1423:1872" class="content-stretch flex gap-[139px] items-start relative shrink-0"><div data-node-id="1423:1873" class="content-stretch flex items-center p-[10px] relative shrink-0"><div data-node-id="1423:1874" data-name="whiterabbit1983_monumental_Brutal_Lenticular_art_installation_9eabb464-f996-4a13-80a0-faff0115beb3_0 1" class="h-[1193px] relative shrink-0 w-[949px]" data-video-hash="f728db3bf56017330509e773f61afc96c4219c69" data-media-status="original-required"></div></div><div data-node-id="1423:1875" class="content-stretch flex flex-col items-center justify-center relative shrink-0 w-[342px]"><div data-node-id="1423:1878" style="font-feature-settings:&quot;lnum&quot; 1, &quot;tnum&quot; 1;--motion-design-transform:initial;--motion-design-transform-inverse:initial;--motion-layout-transform:translate(0px)" class="[text-box-edge:cap_alphabetic] [text-box-trim:trim-both] [word-break:break-word] font-['Space_Mono'] leading-[0] not-italic relative shrink-0 text-[16px] text-black w-full whitespace-pre-wrap"><p class="leading-[1.6] mb-0">I imagined the images at architectural scale, separated across multiple planes of glass. Individual fragments would occupy different depths so that the portrait could align from one position and begin to fracture as the viewer moved. The image would no longer be fixed. Where you stood would determine what you saw.</p><p class="leading-[1.6] mb-0">​</p><p class="leading-[1.6] mb-0">At this scale the body becomes part of the work. A person approaching the installation sees one image; walking alongside it reveals another. Faces separate, overlap and momentarily reconnect. Reflections introduce people and architecture into the composition. Afternoon sunlight passes through the layers, projecting fragments onto the ground and changing the work again as the day moves.</p><p class="leading-[1.6] mb-0">The glass is therefore not simply a surface for displaying the image. It is part of the image.</p><p class="leading-[1.6] mb-0">For years these works existed only as compositions on a screen. Reconstructing the idea as an installation finally revealed the scale I had imagined for them. The portrait becomes less like an image and more like a piece of architecture — something that can be approached, walked around and experienced physically.</p><p class="leading-[1.6]">The work is completed by movement, light, space and the presence of other people.</p></div></div></div></SourceComposition>
