x]">[A concise editorial account. Suitable for smaller projects, illustrations, one-off identities or collaboration.]</p><div data-node-id="1536:3679" class="font-['Geist_Mono'] leading-[0] relative shrink-0 text-[86.733px] text-black tracking-[-1.7347px] w-[427px] whitespace-pre-wrap"><p class="leading-[0.89] mb-0">a girl and </p><p class="leading-[0.89]">a dog eating cake.</p></div></div></div><div data-node-id="1536:3680" class="[word-break:break-word] font-['American_Typewriter'] leading-[0] not-italic relative shrink-0 text-[18px] text-black tracking-[0.54px] w-[953px] whitespace-pre-wrap"><p class="leading-[1.4] mb-0">its a difficult image to process and i am tempted to get rid of it ...but it would look good on a t-shirt. </p><p class="leading-[1.4]">the more i look at it the more i realize why its so powerful. Its interesting to see how a computer randomly interprets a piece of cake, a girl and a dog eating cake.. and the best part for me is simply how simple the prompt was, just , a girl and a dog eating cake. This is thee mid journey, and over the last few years i have been unable to resist the shift of technology.. and now tools have become so powerful they are apparently escaping from the computers in which they were made. Well im not surprised.  Back to the point, which there is none .. the level of uncertainty is so extreme that its almost impossible to know whats next, or how difficult it is to maintain a singular direction.. I have to stay focused on the prompt .. the end result, and thi is often felt relatively quickly .. but sometimes longer and whats most astonishing is the fact that there are a an infinite number of possibilities when it comes to really pixels displaying a number of sets. I think strength is the most elegant   </p></div></div></SourceComposition>
<section class="source-text source-copy project-section" style="--source-space:96px;--source-height:99px" data-source-section="1476:974"><div data-node-id="1476:974" data-name="Copy / FROM DRAWING TO OBJECT" class="content-stretch flex gap-[60px] items-start relative shrink-0 w-[1344px]"><div data-node-id="I1476:974;1112:493" data-name="Label" class="content-stretch flex flex-col items-start relative shrink-0 w-[330px]"><p data-node-id="I1476:974;1112:494" class="[word-break:break-word] font-['Hanken_Grotesk'] font-bold font-bold leading-[1.6] relative shrink-0 text-[color:#0a0a0a] text-[length:12px] tracking-[0.96px] uppercase w-[300px]">FROM DRAWING TO OBJECT</p></div><div data-node-id="I1476:974;1112:495" data-name="Copy" class="content-stretch flex flex-col items-start relative shrink-0 w-[954px]"><p data-node-id="I1476:974;1112:496" class="[word-break:break-word] font-['Hanken_Grotesk'] font-normal leading-[1.5] relative shrink-0 text-[length:22px] text-black w-[930px]">The illustrated textile studies carry the drawn line into a different material. Cropped figures, repeating marks and woven surfaces extend the same interest in image and pattern beyond the flat composition.</p></div></div></section>
</div>
